{% set local_problem %}
<p>Arab sits at the edge of the Appalachian Plateau, surrounded by beautiful hardwood forests. Those trees provide shade and scenery — but they also drop an enormous amount of debris onto your roof and into your gutters. Oak leaves, pine needles, maple seeds, and twigs accumulate constantly from spring through fall.</p>

<p>Without gutter guards, Arab homeowners face a never-ending maintenance cycle. Gutters clog, water overflows, and you're back on the ladder again. On hillside lots, clogged gutters are especially dangerous — overflow water concentrates and flows downhill toward your foundation at increased velocity.</p>

<p>Many Arab homeowners tell us they clean their gutters 3-4 times per year, or pay $150-200 each time for professional cleaning. Over just a few years, those costs add up to more than a quality gutter guard system that solves the problem permanently.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Gutter guards are protective covers or screens that fit over or inside your gutters, allowing water to enter while blocking leaves, pine needles, twigs, and other debris. They work by creating a barrier that debris can't penetrate while still allowing rainwater to flow through.</p>

<p>Different types work better for different debris. Micro-mesh guards excel at blocking fine debris like pine needles and roof grit — ideal for Arab's mix of hardwoods and pines. Screen guards handle larger leaves effectively. Solid covers use surface tension to pull water in while debris slides off.</p>

<p>We'll assess your home's specific situation — which trees surround it, your roof pitch, and your existing gutter system — and recommend the best solution.</p>
{% endset %}

